TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Orbit; Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Image 1; Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Contents; Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"RAAN is not determined by the launch facility location alone; its value must be tied to a specified epoch and target orbital plane. The listed elements also omit an epoch, so the initial orbit is not fully specified."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"A sun-synchronous orbit does not keep the spacecraft continuously sunlit. Eclipse periods can occur, and the descending portion is not inherently the sunlit portion; local solar time and season determine illumination."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report does not specify sensor spectral bands, ground resolution, swath, pointing, or revisit performance, so it does not demonstrate that a 600 km orbit and the proposed imaging concept can support the required reef monitoring."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"The equatorial launch advantage is overstated for a near-polar retrograde orbit, and the claimed two daily launch windows are not established by comparing launch-site latitude with inclination alone; target-plane alignment and launch azimuth matter."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The Mount Pleasant Radio Observatory is identified, but the report does not establish that it has compatible spacecraft TT&C equipment, frequencies, or operational arrangements. The access results alone do not establish a functioning ground-control service."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The report first calls EOL the only orbital manoeuvre, then says the spacecraft has no thrusters and proposes a passive drag sail. A drag sail is a passive deorbit device, not a commanded orbital manoeuvre; its size and expected deorbit time are not calculated."},{"where":"Launch; Bibliography","issue":"The report relies on a Wikipedia spaceflight calendar despite the task explicitly prohibiting Wikipedia, and launch-table citations such as [49] and [50]–[74] have no corresponding bibliography entries."}],"overall":"Weighted result: 49/100. The mission is clearly motivated and uses STK evidence, but important operational and spacecraft-performance assumptions remain unverified, and the report has citation and presentation deficiencies.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The report explains the coral-monitoring mission, its Australian environmental rationale, and why one spacecraft is considered sufficient. It identifies likely science and government customers, but does not clearly designate a spacecraft operator. The concept is understandable, though the user and operational roles are not developed in detail.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report covers launch, orbit, ground access, mission duration and disposal, and includes STK imagery, access data and an inclination calculation. However, the design is not fully substantiated: payload performance and coverage are not specified, orbital elements are incomplete, the ground-station capability is assumed, and sunlight and EOL claims are inconsistent. No STK project files are supplied.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost ten minutes was achieved, in addition to the already frequent amount of passes."},{"where":"Image 1","quote":""},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion recaps the selected launch, orbit and ground facility, and notes uncertainty about the proposed launcher. It connects the concept to reef protection, but mainly summarizes choices rather than drawing detailed conclusions from the design evidence or acknowledging its limitations.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"Thus, launch may need to be adjusted if the rocket is still not constructed by the proposed date."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a recognizable structure, contents, summary, conclusion and bibliography, and is generally readable. However, grammar and wording errors remain; figures and tables are not consistently numbered and captioned; references are incomplete, and some in-text citations have no matching bibliography entries. The cited Wikipedia source is not acceptable under the task instructions.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"DN path for orbits 1-15"}]}]}
